<Base title="Run a node" description="Run a khensu.nu node on a Raspberry Pi 5, an old phone, or your computer in the background.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Run a node</p>
  <h1>Be part of the network</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A node needs a connection and a power socket, nothing more. It forwards traffic it can’t read, keeps encrypted
    mailboxes for people who are offline, and earns for the work it can prove it did.
  </p>

  <h2>Hardware</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Old or unused phone</td><td>The best way to run a node, and the most profitable: you already own it, so there’s nothing to buy and what it earns is yours from day one. Leave it on Wi-Fi and plugged in, with khensu.nu open in node mode. An iPhone 15 Pro on 2 Gbit fibre over Wi-Fi 6E, sharing half, carries about 3,400 people a day.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your laptop or desktop</td><td>Runs a node in the background while you work, using 50% or less of its idle processing power, on a Mac today.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/raspberry-pi-5/">Raspberry Pi 5</a></td><td>A small, quiet computer that uses about as much power as a light bulb. On gigabit fibre, sharing half its connection, it carries about 1,700 people a day.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Connection</h2>
  <p>Fibre, cable, 5G home internet or satellite internet. A node on satellite internet is a full node. Where there’s no internet at all, nodes nearby reach each other directly.</p>

